-- title:
--   Bi-fibre count over a linearly disjoint compositum
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and let $F, F_1, F_2, E, M$ be fields equipped with $K$-algebra structures and with algebra maps making up the towers $K \subseteq F \subseteq F_1, F_2 \subseteq E \subseteq M$ (all the relevant scalar-tower compatibilities being imposed), and assume $F_1/F$, $F_2/F$, $E/F$, $E/F_1$, $E/F_2$ and $M/F$ are finite with $M/F$ Galois; $M$ is an auxiliary parameter that does not occur in the conclusion. Assume further that the $F$-subalgebra of $E$ generated by the union of the images of $F_1$ and of $F_2$ is all of $E$ (hypothesis `hgen`), and that $[E:F] = [F_1:F]\,[F_2:F]$ (hypothesis `hLD`). Here a place of a $K$-algebra field $F'$ is a valuation subring of $F'$ containing the image of $K$, different from $F'$, and a principal ideal ring; restriction of a place along an algebra map is the preimage valuation subring; for a place $W$ of $F'$ over a subfield $F''$, $e(W/F'')$ is the least positive integer $n$ for which some nonzero $f \in F''$ has $W$-order $n$ in $F'$, and $f(W/F'') = [\,\kappa(W) : \kappa(W|_{F''})\,]$, the degree of the residue field of $W$ over that of its restriction. Given a place $v$ of $F$, places $w_1$ of $F_1$ and $w_2$ of $F_2$ both restricting to $v$, and a finite set $T$ of places of $E$ whose members are exactly the places $W$ of $E$ with $W|_{F_1} = w_1$ and $W|_{F_2} = w_2$, the conclusion is $$\sum_{W \in T} e(W/F)\,f(W/F) = \bigl(e(w_1/F)\,f(w_1/F)\bigr)\bigl(e(w_2/F)\,f(w_2/F)\bigr).$$
--
--   This is the combinatorial core of the local exchange identity for places of a compositum of two extensions that are linearly disjoint over the base: dividing by $e(w_2/v)f(w_1/v)$ using the multiplicativity of $e$ and $f$ in towers turns it into $\sum_{W \mid w_1, w_2} e(W/w_2)f(W/w_1) = e(w_1/v)f(w_2/v)$. It is used in the proof of [`AlgebraicCurve.Place.sum_ramificationIndex_mul_inertiaDeg_exchange`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.Place.sum_ramificationIndex_mul_inertiaDeg_exchange), which compares pushforward and pullback of divisors along the two sides of the square $F \subseteq F_1, F_2 \subseteq E$.
